      "Language": "chi",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate the long-term outcomes of weight loss and the cause of high rate of loss to follow-up after laparoscopic adjustable gastric banding (LAGB) in obese patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Clinical and follow-up data of 226 obese patients undergoing LAGB operation in the Changhai Hospital from June 2003 to June 2007 were analyzed retrospectively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "A total of 125 patients were followed up for 3-7 years. Among these 125 cases, 115 (92.0%) presented weight loss after LAGB, 60 (48.0%) presented extra weight loss after LAGB. Another 105 cases (44.7%) were lost to follow-up. Most of patients who were loss to follow-up were younger (P<0.05). Gender, weight and BMI were not associated with the rate of loss to follow-up (all P>0.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Laparoscopic adjustable gastric band surgery is a relatively simple and safe procedure among the bariatric surgery, but the follow-up protocol is complex and the rate of loss to follow-up is high. Postoperative follow-up should be emphasized."
        }
      ]},
